## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
<h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Tech-Free or Tech Bound?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify complex text for peers. Communication: Mediate between peers in conflict.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can mediate simple conflicts about technology use by using gestures to indicate "balance" (e.g., hands moving like a scale to show "balance" between tech and no-tech activities). Can simplify explanations about the impact of technology by using images (e.g., pointing to a "no phone" sign when discussing screen time limits). Can use gestures and simple language to mediate discussions about the role of technology (e.g., motioning a "cut-off" hand for reducing screen time).</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students watch a short silent video or look at a series of images showing common tech-related conflicts (e.g., a family dinner with everyone on phones, friends arguing over screen time, someone missing out on real-life activities because of a device). Teacher asks: "What problem do you see?" "How do the people feel?" "What *could* be a *solution*?" (5 min)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher explains that today we will learn to 'mediate' conflicts and simplify explanations about technology. Teacher shows two students (or puppets/drawings) having a disagreement about screen time. Teacher steps in and models mediation: "Okay, let's find a *balance*. You *could* spend 30 minutes on your game, and then you *should* *disconnect* and *interact* with friends. It’s *easier* to focus." Teacher explicitly uses hand gestures for "balance" (like a scale) and "cut-off" (hand motion). (5 min)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'mediate' mean? Is it taking sides or helping everyone?" (Expected answer: Helping everyone.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'find a *solution*', what am I trying to do?" (Expected answer: Find an answer to the problem.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can gestures like 'balance' help us mediate?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3 min)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">(13 min)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue or a simplified blog post excerpt (genre: informal advice, register: peer-to-peer/guidance, format: text) describing a student's struggle with the "Technology-Free Day Challenge" and a friend's unsympathetic reaction. For example: "My friend Carla said, 'I *have been* so bored *for* five hours without my *cellphone* during the challenge. It was *more difficult* than I thought!' Her friend David laughed and said, 'You just need to *disconnect*! It's *easier* to be *focused* without your *device*.' Carla looked sad." (5 min)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Discuss the positive and negative aspects of technology in a guided classroom debate, proposing solutions for overuse. This stage sets up a scenario for students to practice mediating such a discussion. (2 min)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is Carla's main problem?" "How does David make Carla feel?" "Is David being *helpful*?" (3 min)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements and decide if they are true or false based on the text. E.g., "Carla found the challenge *easier*." (False). "David offered a *meaningful* *solution*." (False). (3 min)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">(13 min)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Modals ("can," "could," "should") for ability and suggestions. Comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"). Present perfect ("since," "for").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused, often, usually, actively.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice production of minimal pairs (/θ/ and /ð/ sounds in words like "path" vs. "father," "think" vs. "this"); identification and pronunciation of past tense verbs ending "-ed" (/d/ in "saved," /ɪd/ in "disconnected," /t/ in "liked").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Giving opinions ("I think technology is useful"), Discussing pros and cons ("Digital technology makes communication faster and easier, but it can be distracting"), Offering suggestions ("You could try using less screen time").</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ol>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion & Gestures:</b> Students complete sentences with appropriate modals or comparatives, related to mediation. E.g., "Carla _____ (could/should) try to *find* other *activities*." "It's _____ (easier/more difficult) to *interact* when you put your *device* away." After completing, they practice saying the sentence with an accompanying mediation gesture (e.g., 'balance' for finding activities, 'cut-off' for putting devices away). Teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /θ/ and /ð/ sounds and -ed endings where applicable. (5 min)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Suggestion Chain:</b> In small groups, students take turns identifying a negative aspect of technology use (e.g., "I *have been* *distracted* by *social media* *for* two hours") and then offering a mediation-focused suggestion using modals and target vocabulary (e.g., "You *could* *reduce* your screen time by *actively* doing something else," "You *should* *disconnect* your *device*"). Encourage varied vocabulary like "benefits," "solution." (5 min)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Simplifying the Challenge:</b> Students work in pairs. One student pretends to be a classmate who was absent during the explanation of the "Technology-Free Day Challenge" or is confused about its *benefits*. The other student must simplify the explanation of the challenge and its purpose, using simple language, gestures (e.g., pointing to an imaginary "no phone" sign), and target vocabulary (e.g., "It's about *reducing* your *technology use*. You *can* *save* time and *interact* *more* with friends. It's *easier* to be *focused*."). The "absent" student provides feedback on clarity. (7 min)</li>
        </ol>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">(17 min)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Three-person role-play: "Mediating the Technology-Free Day Challenge Reflection."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form groups of three. Each group receives a scenario card related to the "Technology-Free Day Challenge" project.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student A:</b> Expresses frustration or difficulty experienced during the challenge (e.g., "I *have been* so *distracted* by *not using* my *cellphone* *for* the whole day. It was *more difficult* to pass the time.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student B:</b> Expresses a contrasting or unsympathetic view (e.g., "Come on, it's *easier* to be *focused* without your *device*. You *should* just try *actively* to find other *benefits*.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student C (Mediator):</b> Must mediate the discussion using simple language, gestures (balance, cut-off, thinking), and offering *solutions* and suggestions. The mediator *should* use modals ("can," "could," "should"), comparatives, and present perfect. They aim to help A and B *understand* each other and find a *solution* or compromise. E.g., "I understand Student A found it *difficult*, but Student B has a point about *focus*. Student A, you *could* try finding a *helpful* new hobby. Student B, you *should* try to understand it's *more difficult* for some people to *disconnect*."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups prepare their role-play for 5 minutes, focusing on incorporating linguistic competences and mediating gestures. They perform for the class or their peers. (7 min)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">(12 min)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist (for mediator) and peer assessment (for mediator's effectiveness).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The mediator from each group presents their proposed *solution* or summary of the mediated discussion to the class, explaining *how* they helped their peers *understand* each other's perspectives regarding the "Technology-Free Day Challenge." They must use gestures and simple language. (5 min)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of gestures for mediation (e.g., 'balance', 'cut-off', 'think').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplification of explanations/suggestions.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target grammar (modals: "can," "could," "should"; comparatives: "easier," "more difficult"; present perfect: "since," "for").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of theme-related vocabulary (technology, cellphone, solution, benefits, reduce, disconnect, interact, focused, distracted).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /θ/ and /ð/ sounds and -ed endings (e.g., "liked," "saved").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to facilitate communication and propose a *solution*.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two questions:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was *easier* about mediating a discussion this time, and what was *more difficult*?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *can* using gestures help you *explain* things better when mediating?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">(10 min)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn about helping others *understand* different points of view about technology and the 'Technology-Free Day Challenge'?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *can* I use my new mediation skills (e.g., simplifying, using gestures, offering *solutions*) in other real-life situations, beyond the classroom?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked particularly well in helping students mediate and simplify information? (e.g., specific gestures, the role-play scenarios, the use of comparatives).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further develop their mediation skills, especially concerning more complex texts or deeper conflicts?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully integrate the target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation into their mediation tasks?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This is the final lesson of the "Tech-Free or Tech Bound?" unit, concluding the "Technology-Free Day Challenge" project. The next unit will introduce a new theme and set of linguistic objectives, building upon the communicative skills developed here, especially the ability to explain, summarize, and facilitate communication.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">(Approx. 5 min beyond lesson time)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>